Title: Nonclassicality, quasiprobabilities and weak values explored in neutron interferometry


Abstract: 
The violation of Bell inequalities has demonstrated that quantum mechanics exhibits features with no classical counterpart; however, identifying the boundary between quantumness and classicality remains a nontrivial task. Quantumness is a central resource for both fundamental research and technologies such as quantum computing and communication. Therefore, tools to distinguish between classical and quantum features are of great importance. Quasiprobability representations, such as the well-known Wigner function, provide one such approach. More specifically, they provide a map from the Hilbert space formulation of quantum mechanics onto objects that resemble classical probabilities. If a given experiment admits a classical description, its quasiprobability representation will also be classical. Otherwise, nonclassicality manifests itself through anomalous features such as negative or even complex-valued probabilities. It has been shown that weak values are equivalent to conditional quasiprobabilities and therefore they can be used to probe nonclassical phenomena. Their experimental relevance has been confirmed across a range of impactful experiments, several implemented in neutron interferometry. Neutron interferometry allows for fundational studies of quantum mechanics under conditions that are less susceptible to classical interpretations compared to photonic setups; a single massive particle in a superposition of two or three macroscopically separated paths. This talk will include an introduction to classical probability models (hidden-variable models), quasiprobability representations and weak values, and nonclassicality explored in neutron interferometry experiments.

The violation of Bell inequalities has demonstrated that quantum mechanics exhibits features with no classical counterpart; however, identifying the boundary between quantumness and classicality remains a nontrivial task. Quasiprobability representations, such as the well-known Wigner function, provide an approach do investigate such boundaries, by mapping the Hilbert space formulation of quantum mechanics onto objects that resemble classical probabilities. If a given experiment admits a classical description, its quasiprobability representation will also be classical. Otherwise, nonclassicality manifests itself through anomalous features such as negative or even complex-valued probabilities. In this talk, a method to experimentally access quasiprobabilities is introduced and its experimental verification is presented. Specifically, the real and imaginary part of the Kirkwood-Dirac quasiprobability distribution are extracted in a two-path generalized Mach–Zehnder interferometer and in a three-path interferometer. Diffrently from the conventional extraction that relies on weak measurements, our method employs neither meter states nor weak interactions. The experiment is performed in neutron interferometry, a matter-wave platform that employs a single massive particle in a superposition of spatially separated paths. Additionally, owing to the fermionic nature of the neutron, the observed interference is intrinsically a single-particle phenomenon. Compared with typical photonic implementations, neutron interferometry is less susceptible to classical reinterpretations of the results.

